1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,174
Last time, the harbormaster called,

2
00:00:02,624 --> 00:00:06,102
and all the crew could do was listen.

3
00:00:07,002 --> 00:00:09,176
Today they get to answer.

4
00:00:10,076 --> 00:00:12,685
One answer asks her for more,

5
00:00:13,135 --> 00:00:14,874
one takes a job,

6
00:00:15,324 --> 00:00:17,063
and one hangs up.

7
00:00:18,863 --> 00:00:22,341
And the answer they give changes the story.

8
00:00:23,541 --> 00:00:25,715
An answer is one line,

9
00:00:26,165 --> 00:00:28,774
and you already know its shape.

10
00:00:29,674 --> 00:00:32,283
It's a dash and a space,

11
00:00:32,733 --> 00:00:35,341
then the words in square brackets,

12
00:00:35,791 --> 00:00:38,835
then round brackets, with no space between.

13
00:00:39,735 --> 00:00:42,343
It's a heading's name and key,

14
00:00:42,793 --> 00:00:45,837
with a dash where the hashes go.

15
00:00:47,637 --> 00:00:51,550
The square brackets hold what Comms reads and picks.

16
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:56,363
The round brackets say where the conversation goes next,

17
00:00:56,813 --> 00:01:00,291
and when they're empty, the call is over.

18
00:01:01,491 --> 00:01:05,404
Now I put a key in those round brackets,

19
00:01:05,854 --> 00:01:09,767
the key of a scene I haven't written yet.

20
00:01:10,667 --> 00:01:12,841
And lint tells me so:

21
00:01:13,291 --> 00:01:16,770
this answer points at something that isn't there.

22
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:22,917
So I write it, at the end of the file.

23
00:01:23,817 --> 00:01:28,165
It's a scene like the first one, with three hashes,

24
00:01:28,615 --> 00:01:32,528
but it has no When line and no title.

25
00:01:33,428 --> 00:01:37,341
Nothing in the story places it as a call.

26
00:01:38,241 --> 00:01:42,589
The only way in is the answer that names it.

27
00:01:43,789 --> 00:01:48,137
A conversation can branch, and it can come back together.

28
00:01:49,037 --> 00:01:53,385
I add an answer that asks her what she knows,

29
00:01:53,835 --> 00:01:56,878
and I write the scene for it.

30
00:01:57,778 --> 00:02:02,561
And from there, one answer leads on to the same offer.

31
00:02:04,361 --> 00:02:08,709
So a crew that asks first hears a little more,

32
00:02:09,159 --> 00:02:12,202
and ends up in the same place.

33
00:02:13,102 --> 00:02:17,450
Just don't write more than four answers in a scene,

34
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:20,509
because a fifth is never offered.

35
00:02:21,709 --> 00:02:24,752
So far an answer moves the conversation,

36
00:02:25,202 --> 00:02:26,941
or it ends it.

37
00:02:27,841 --> 00:02:31,320
Now one is going to change the story.

38
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:35,263
First I need the job she's offering,

39
00:02:35,713 --> 00:02:39,626
so I write it up here among my quests.

40
00:02:40,526 --> 00:02:42,265
It starts when revealed,

41
00:02:42,715 --> 00:02:46,193
which keeps it hidden until something starts it.

42
00:02:47,993 --> 00:02:50,602
Lint warns me that nothing does,

43
00:02:51,052 --> 00:02:53,226
and look what it lists:

44
00:02:53,676 --> 00:02:56,285
a Then line, or an answer.

45
00:02:57,185 --> 00:03:00,663
So lint already knows where this is going.

46
00:03:01,563 --> 00:03:04,172
I go back to the offer,

47
00:03:04,622 --> 00:03:08,535
and after the round brackets I type a semicolon,

48
00:03:08,985 --> 00:03:11,593
then accepts, and the job's key.

49
00:03:12,493 --> 00:03:16,407
Everything after that semicolon is what the answer does.

50
00:03:17,607 --> 00:03:20,215
An answer can do two things,

51
00:03:20,665 --> 00:03:24,143
with one semicolon, and a comma between them.

52
00:03:25,043 --> 00:03:28,522
Whichever way the crew answers, they've answered her,

53
00:03:28,972 --> 00:03:32,450
so I mark that moment done as well.

54
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:38,598
There are four words you can write after the semicolon.

55
00:03:39,498 --> 00:03:41,237
Accepts starts a job,

56
00:03:41,687 --> 00:03:44,296
completes finishes one and pays it,

57
00:03:44,746 --> 00:03:46,920
fails does what it says,

58
00:03:47,370 --> 00:03:50,848
and signal sends a word into the story.

59
00:03:52,048 --> 00:03:56,396
What the crew said can come back to them later.

60
00:03:57,296 --> 00:04:02,078
When the beacon is set, I want her to call again,

61
00:04:02,528 --> 00:04:04,702
and that takes three pieces.

62
00:04:05,602 --> 00:04:09,950
Watch lint walk me from each one to the next.

63
00:04:11,750 --> 00:04:14,793
First, a Then line on the job,

64
00:04:15,243 --> 00:04:19,157
and lint says it reveals something that isn't there.

65
00:04:20,057 --> 00:04:23,970
So I write that, a beat like last time's,

66
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:29,202
and lint says the beat calls a scene that isn't there.

67
00:04:30,102 --> 00:04:34,015
So I write the scene, and now it's clean.

68
00:04:35,815 --> 00:04:38,424
And here's the point of it.

69
00:04:39,324 --> 00:04:43,672
Only a crew that took the job, and finished it,

70
00:04:44,122 --> 00:04:45,861
ever hears this call.

71
00:04:47,061 --> 00:04:52,278
So that's two ways to make an answer depend on the story.

72
00:04:53,178 --> 00:04:58,396
You put it in a scene that only one answer leads to,

73
00:04:58,846 --> 00:05:02,324
or in a call that only comes later.

74
00:05:04,124 --> 00:05:07,602
You'll see a third way in other pages,

75
00:05:08,052 --> 00:05:11,096
a condition written on the answer itself.

76
00:05:11,996 --> 00:05:14,604
Don't use it in this mission.

77
00:05:15,504 --> 00:05:18,983
It can't read your quests or your credits,

78
00:05:19,433 --> 00:05:22,476
so the answer would never be offered,

79
00:05:22,926 --> 00:05:24,665
and lint can't tell.

80
00:05:25,565 --> 00:05:28,609
We'll use it properly in Class 3.

81
00:05:29,809 --> 00:05:32,852
In we go, and here's her call.

82
00:05:33,752 --> 00:05:35,491
She asks her question,

83
00:05:35,941 --> 00:05:39,854
and now there are three answers in the list.

84
00:05:40,754 --> 00:05:43,363
I'll ask what she knows first,

85
00:05:43,813 --> 00:05:47,291
then I tell her the hulk is cold,

86
00:05:47,741 --> 00:05:49,480
and there's the offer.

87
00:05:51,280 --> 00:05:53,020
We will tag her.

88
00:05:53,920 --> 00:05:55,659
The call is over,

89
00:05:56,109 --> 00:05:59,587
and the job is in the Quest Log.

90
00:06:00,487 --> 00:06:03,530
Thirty seconds later it's done and paid,

91
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:05,720
and she's calling back.

92
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:08,659
Now the other roads.

93
00:06:09,559 --> 00:06:13,037
If I tell her to find someone else,

94
00:06:13,487 --> 00:06:16,530
there's no job, and no second call.

95
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:20,039
And if I say not now,

96
00:06:20,489 --> 00:06:24,402
the call is gone, and it doesn't come back.

97
00:06:26,202 --> 00:06:28,811
But Back is a different thing.

98
00:06:29,711 --> 00:06:33,624
It puts the call back in the list, unanswered,

99
00:06:34,074 --> 00:06:36,683
and nothing in the story changes.

100
00:06:37,583 --> 00:06:41,496
So the crew always has a way to wait.

101
00:06:42,396 --> 00:06:45,439
Only write an answer that hangs up

102
00:06:45,889 --> 00:06:49,802
when your story can go on without the call.

103
00:06:51,002 --> 00:06:55,350
Now it's your turn: give your second call some answers.

104
00:06:56,250 --> 00:07:00,598
Make one of them send a word into the story,

105
00:07:01,048 --> 00:07:04,961
and write a quest that waits for that word.

106
00:07:06,761 --> 00:07:11,978
Next time, a step the crew can only finish by reporting in.
